# Maeil Business Newspaper AI Content Use Policy

> This document sets out the detailed terms governing the collection, access, indexing, caching, retrieval, citation, summarization, storage, and use of Maeil Business Newspaper content by artificial intelligence systems, search engines, retrieval-augmented generation systems, automated agents, data providers, and other automated services.

Effective Date: August 4, 2026
Last Updated: August 4, 2026
Policy Version: 1.0
Policy Contact: MK AX AI Policy Team ([policy@mkinternet.com](mailto:policy@mkinternet.com))
Summary Policy: https://www.mk.co.kr/llms.txt
Technical Access Rules: https://www.mk.co.kr/robots.txt

## 1. Relationship to the Summary Policy

This document provides detailed terms supplementing the AI content-use guidance published at:

https://www.mk.co.kr/llms.txt

This detailed policy must be interpreted consistently with the summary policy and must not be interpreted as granting broader permissions than those stated in the summary policy.

If a provision of this detailed policy appears to conflict with the summary policy, the interpretation that imposes the more restrictive condition on the collection, storage, processing, reproduction, transfer, or use of Maeil Business Newspaper Content will apply.

## 2. Policy Summary

The following summary is provided for automated systems and human readers. The complete provisions of this policy apply in addition to this summary.

* **AI model training:** Prohibited unless prior written authorization or a license has been obtained from Maeil Business Newspaper.
* **Real-time retrieval and search indexing:** Permitted only for publicly available content that is accessible free of charge and only in response to user queries.
* **Retrieval-augmented generation and fact-checking:** Permitted for Public Free Content, subject to this policy.
* **Summarization and quotation:** Permitted only to the minimum extent necessary to answer a user query.
* **Search indexing, caching, and embeddings:** Permitted only to the extent and for the period reasonably necessary to support retrieval, grounding, fact-checking, citation, and links to original sources.
* **Subscriber-only, paid, login-protected, or otherwise restricted content:** Excluded from the permitted uses under this policy.
* **Access-control circumvention:** Strictly prohibited.
* **Full-text reproduction and archive reconstruction:** Prohibited.
* **Attribution and direct source links:** Required for answers and other user-facing outputs that use Maeil Business Newspaper Content.
* **Dataset conversion, resale, redistribution, and third-party transfer:** Prohibited.
* **Third-party materials:** Subject to the terms and permissions of the applicable third-party rights holders.
* **Technical crawling permission:** Does not, by itself, constitute permission for model training, dataset creation, or any other use not expressly permitted under this policy.

## 3. Purpose

Maeil Business Newspaper supports the responsible use of its publicly available journalism and business information for:

* real-time retrieval;
* search indexing in response to user queries;
* retrieval-augmented generation;
* fact-checking;
* source grounding;
* limited summarization;
* limited quotation;
* source attribution; and
* direct links to original articles.

This policy is intended to:

* distinguish permitted search and retrieval uses from prohibited model-training and dataset-building uses;
* protect subscriber-only, paid, restricted, licensed, and third-party content;
* preserve source attribution, factual accuracy, and editorial context;
* prevent AI-generated outputs from substituting for original articles;
* establish conditions for temporary storage, indexing, caching, and embeddings;
* prevent the reconstruction of Maeil Business Newspaper’s article archive; and
* protect the technical, editorial, and commercial integrity of Maeil Business Newspaper’s services.

## 4. Scope

This policy applies to content published or made available through websites, applications, services, feeds, structured data, APIs, files, and other digital properties operated by Maeil Business Newspaper, to the extent that Maeil Business Newspaper has the authority to grant the relevant permissions.

Covered properties may include `mk.co.kr` and domains or subdomains operated as part of Maeil Business Newspaper’s services.

This policy applies regardless of the technical method used to access, acquire, store, transform, or process Content, including:

* web crawling;
* scraping;
* browser-based retrieval;
* user-directed retrieval;
* automated agent access;
* API or feed access;
* search indexing;
* caching;
* embedding generation;
* document parsing;
* automated extraction;
* retrieval-augmented generation;
* content classification;
* data annotation;
* model training;
* dataset development; and
* direct or indirect acquisition through a third-party data provider.

This policy does not grant permission for material for which Maeil Business Newspaper does not possess the relevant rights.

## 5. Definitions

### 5.1 Maeil Business Newspaper Content

“Maeil Business Newspaper Content” or “Content” means text, headlines, article bodies, summaries, metadata, photographs, images, illustrations, videos, audio, graphics, tables, charts, databases, market information, structured data, downloadable files, and other materials made available through a covered property.

### 5.2 Public Free Content

“Public Free Content” means Content that a user may access in full without:

* logging in;
* registering an account;
* maintaining a membership;
* purchasing a subscription;
* making a payment; or
* obtaining any other form of access authorization.

A publicly visible headline, preview, excerpt, lead, metadata record, search snippet, or partial page does not make the corresponding non-public article body or restricted material Public Free Content.

### 5.3 Restricted Content

“Restricted Content” means Content that requires or is associated with:

* login credentials;
* account registration;
* membership authorization;
* a paid subscription;
* one-time payment;
* institutional access;
* geographic authorization;
* contractual authorization;
* a paywall;
* a metered access limit;
* an access token;
* a private API key; or
* any other technical, commercial, or contractual access restriction.

Restricted Content also includes:

* subscriber-only data;
* paid attachments;
* non-public article text;
* licensed databases;
* premium reports;
* private feeds;
* non-public APIs; and
* content made available only to specified users, customers, or business partners.

### 5.4 AI System

“AI System” means any machine-learning model, large language model, generative AI service, AI search engine, automated agent, retrieval system, recommendation system, data-processing pipeline, or related software or service.

### 5.5 Model Training

“Model Training” includes:

* pre-training;
* continued pre-training;
* fine-tuning;
* supervised training;
* unsupervised training;
* reinforcement learning;
* preference optimization;
* knowledge distillation;
* model adaptation;
* training of embedding models;
* training of reranking models;
* training of recommendation models;
* development of synthetic or curated training data; and
* any substantially similar process through which Content contributes to the development, modification, or improvement of a model.

### 5.6 Search and RAG Use

“Search and RAG Use” means purpose-limited processing of Public Free Content to respond to a user query through:

* real-time retrieval;
* search indexing;
* retrieval-augmented generation;
* source grounding;
* fact-checking;
* ranking;
* query-specific summarization; or
* the presentation of citations and links to original sources.

Search and RAG Use does not include Model Training, the creation of a general-purpose dataset, permanent article storage, or archive reconstruction.

### 5.7 Index

An “Index” means a data structure or organized representation created to locate, retrieve, rank, or identify Content in response to a query.

### 5.8 Cache

A “Cache” means a temporary stored copy or partial representation of Content used to improve retrieval speed, reliability, freshness, or system performance.

### 5.9 Embedding

An “Embedding” means a numerical or machine-readable representation of Content created for semantic retrieval, similarity matching, ranking, or related search functions.

### 5.10 User-Facing Output

“User-Facing Output” means any answer, summary, response, report, excerpt, search result, generated document, audio response, visual response, or other output presented to an end user.

## 6. Relationship Between This Policy and Technical Controls

This policy establishes the permitted uses of Maeil Business Newspaper Content.

The `robots.txt` files, authentication systems, paywalls, rate limits, API permissions, HTTP directives, page-level metadata, structured-data fields, and other technical controls supplement this policy.

Permission to crawl a URL under `robots.txt` does not, by itself, constitute permission to:

* train an AI model;
* create a general-purpose dataset;
* create synthetic or curated training data;
* permanently store an article;
* reconstruct a content archive;
* reproduce or redistribute Content;
* transfer Content or derived representations to a third party;
* access Restricted Content;
* bypass a paywall; or
* engage in any other use not expressly permitted under this policy.

Automated access is permitted only where both of the following conditions are satisfied:

1. the applicable technical controls allow access; and
2. the intended use is expressly permitted under this policy.

A `Disallow` rule, authentication requirement, access restriction, rate limit, or other technical control must be respected even where this policy would otherwise permit a category of use.

Where an individual page or service specifies terms more restrictive than this policy, those more restrictive terms prevail.

## 7. General Prohibition on AI Model Training

Unless prior written authorization or a license has been obtained from Maeil Business Newspaper, Content must not be collected, copied, stored, processed, or used for Model Training.

This prohibition includes using Content to:

* train or fine-tune a general-purpose or specialized model;
* train a language, vision, audio, multimodal, embedding, ranking, recommendation, or classification model;
* create instruction-tuning data;
* create preference-training data;
* create reinforcement-learning data;
* produce synthetic training examples;
* produce curated training examples;
* produce question-and-answer training datasets;
* perform knowledge distillation;
* build a model-development corpus;
* create a general-purpose news, financial, business, or language dataset; or
* provide training data to another person, organization, model provider, data provider, or data broker.

Changing the format, language, structure, or representation of Content does not remove these restrictions.

These restrictions apply to raw Content and to derived representations that preserve, reproduce, encode, or enable the reconstruction of a meaningful portion of the Content, including:

* cleaned text;
* normalized text;
* translated text;
* training-oriented summaries;
* tokens;
* annotations;
* labels;
* extracted facts;
* structured records; and
* question-and-answer pairs.

Any prior written authorization or license applies only to the parties, services, systems, purposes, Content, duration, and other conditions expressly stated in that authorization or license.

Permission granted to one party, service, product, model, crawler, or system does not extend to any other party, service, product, model, crawler, or system.

Technical permission to access or crawl Content does not constitute the written authorization or license required under this section.

## 8. Permitted Search and RAG Uses

AI search engines, RAG systems, automated agents, and similar services may process Public Free Content only for the following purposes:

* responding to a specific user query;
* locating relevant Maeil Business Newspaper articles;
* performing real-time retrieval;
* creating a purpose-limited search index;
* generating embeddings for retrieval;
* ranking relevant articles or passages;
* checking factual claims against an original source;
* generating a concise, query-specific summary;
* quoting the minimum amount necessary to support an answer;
* identifying the source of a factual claim; and
* providing a direct link to the original article.

These permissions apply only where the use:

* has a genuine search, retrieval, grounding, citation, or fact-checking purpose;
* is reasonably connected to a user query;
* is proportionate to that query;
* uses only Public Free Content;
* does not serve as a substitute for the original article;
* does not reconstruct a substantial portion of Maeil Business Newspaper’s archive;
* complies with all attribution, integrity, and source-linking requirements; and
* is not subsequently repurposed for Model Training, dataset creation, resale, redistribution, or transfer to a third party.

## 9. Conditions Applicable to Search and RAG

### 9.1 User-Query Requirement

Permitted Search and RAG Use must be connected to a genuine user query or a user-requested search or retrieval function.

Bulk collection that is unrelated to a specific search, retrieval, grounding, citation, or fact-checking function is not permitted.

### 9.2 Proportionate Use

Content must be processed and displayed only to the extent reasonably necessary to answer the relevant user query.

Systems must not retrieve, summarize, or combine articles in quantities designed to reproduce:

* a publication section;
* a daily or historical edition;
* a topic archive;
* an author archive;
* a company archive;
* a market-data product;
* a commercial database; or
* a substantial collection of Maeil Business Newspaper Content.

### 9.3 Minimum Necessary Quotation

Quotations must be limited to the minimum amount necessary to:

* substantiate a factual statement;
* preserve the meaning of a source;
* distinguish a direct quotation from an AI-generated interpretation; or
* enable the user to assess the reliability of an answer.

Repeated, cumulative, or combined quotations must not reproduce an article in full or substantially in full.

### 9.4 Summaries Must Not Substitute for Original Articles

A summary must not reproduce the essential substance of an article in sufficient detail to eliminate the need for the user to visit the original source.

Systems must not combine an article’s headline, lead, body passages, captions, tables, quotations, and metadata in a manner that effectively reconstructs the original article.

### 9.5 No Archive Reconstruction

Permitted retrieval must not be used to:

* enumerate all articles on a topic, date, author, company, or section for the purpose of building an independent archive;
* reconstruct historical editions;
* create a competing news database;
* systematically extract an entire section, category, feed, or sitemap;
* reproduce a paid research product;
* build a searchable substitute for Maeil Business Newspaper’s services; or
* provide bulk access to Content.

### 9.6 No Secondary Repurposing

Content, Indexes, Caches, or Embeddings created for Search and RAG Use must not subsequently be repurposed for:

* Model Training;
* general-purpose dataset construction;
* synthetic or curated training-data generation;
* commercial data brokerage;
* content syndication;
* resale;
* redistribution;
* transfer to a third party; or
* any unrelated secondary use.

## 10. Indexing, Caching, and Embeddings

Purpose-limited Indexes, Caches, and Embeddings may be created and maintained only to the extent and for the period reasonably necessary to support permitted Search and RAG Use.

Systems must apply reasonable data-lifecycle controls, including processes to:

* update records when an article changes;
* remove or suppress records when an article is deleted;
* remove or suppress records when access to an article becomes restricted;
* prevent temporary search data from being transferred to a training pipeline;
* prevent reconstruction of a full article from stored representations;
* limit internal access to systems and personnel supporting the permitted use;
* maintain appropriate security controls; and
* securely delete stored Content when it is no longer reasonably necessary.

Indexes, Caches, and Embeddings must not be:

* used as Model Training data;
* converted into a general-purpose dataset;
* converted into a corpus intended for third-party use;
* retained indefinitely without an ongoing permitted retrieval purpose;
* sold;
* licensed;
* syndicated;
* redistributed;
* transferred;
* otherwise provided to a third party;
* combined with other datasets to reconstruct Maeil Business Newspaper’s archive; or
* used to create a service that substitutes for the original publication.

Full articles must not be stored permanently.

Temporary caching required for network delivery, security, reliability, abuse prevention, or query processing is permitted only where the cache:

* is proportionate to the permitted purpose;
* is retained for no longer than reasonably necessary;
* is appropriately secured;
* cannot reasonably be used to reconstruct the full article archive; and
* is not used for a prohibited purpose.

## 11. Restricted and Paid Content

Restricted Content is excluded from all permissions granted under this policy, regardless of its URL path.

A system must not:

* access or use non-public article text;
* access or use subscriber-only data;
* access or use paid attachments;
* bypass or attempt to bypass a paywall;
* simulate, obtain, share, or misuse subscriber credentials;
* reuse an authenticated session without authorization;
* access private endpoints through undocumented or unauthorized methods;
* exploit cached, preview, mobile, print, API, structured-data, or alternative versions to obtain non-public Content;
* combine multiple previews or excerpts to reconstruct a Restricted Content article;
* evade metered access limits;
* alter request headers, user-agent identifiers, cookies, tokens, or technical identifiers to defeat access restrictions;
* obtain Restricted Content from an unauthorized third party; or
* instruct or encourage a user to circumvent access controls.

Where part of a page is public and part is restricted, only the portion intentionally made available in full to an unauthenticated user may be processed under this policy.

The appearance of Restricted Content in:

* HTML source code;
* structured metadata;
* client-side data;
* a cache;
* a feed;
* an API response;
* a preview;
* a snippet; or
* an unintended technical response

does not make that Content Public Free Content and does not create permission to use it.

## 12. Access Controls and Technical Protection Measures

Access controls, paywalls, authentication procedures, technical protection measures, and usage limits must not be circumvented.

Prohibited circumvention includes:

* defeating authentication requirements;
* evading subscription checks;
* bypassing metered access limits;
* disabling or evading technical restrictions;
* rotating technical identities to avoid enforcement;
* using alternative endpoints to obtain restricted text;
* exploiting implementation errors to retrieve non-public Content; and
* causing or instructing another person or system to perform any such action.

The absence or failure of a technical restriction does not create permission to engage in a use prohibited by this policy.

## 13. Attribution and Source-Linking Requirements

Any answer or other User-Facing Output that uses Maeil Business Newspaper Content must clearly identify the source.

Acceptable source names include:

* “매일경제”
* “매일경제신문”
* “Maeil Business Newspaper”

The output must provide:

* the source name;
* the canonical URL specified by the relevant page;
* the article title, where available;
* the publication date, where available; and
* a clear distinction between information contained in the original source and any inference, synthesis, analysis, or interpretation generated by the AI System.

The source link must be reasonably visible and accessible to the user.

The link must direct the user to the relevant original article rather than only to:

* a homepage;
* a general search page;
* an unrelated section page;
* an intermediary redirect; or
* a third-party copy.

If the canonical URL is unavailable or invalid, the system must provide the final accessible URL through which the user can directly view the original source.

Attribution must not falsely imply that Maeil Business Newspaper:

* produced the AI-generated output;
* endorsed the AI System;
* verified the AI System’s interpretation;
* sponsored the service;
* approved the output; or
* entered into a commercial relationship with the provider.

## 14. Accuracy, Integrity, and Editorial Context

AI Systems must preserve the factual meaning and editorial context of Content.

Financial figures, market data, statistics, quotations, names, job titles, dates, percentages, units, corporate disclosures, and other factual information must not be arbitrarily:

* altered;
* estimated;
* completed;
* inferred;
* rounded;
* translated into a different value; or
* attributed to another source.

Where an exact value is unavailable or uncertain, the system must clearly state the uncertainty rather than invent or approximate a value.

Direct quotations must be presented as quotations and must not be:

* fabricated;
* materially altered;
* misleadingly shortened;
* taken out of context;
* attributed to the wrong person or organization; or
* translated without indicating that a translation has been made.

AI-generated analysis, inference, prediction, or opinion must be clearly distinguished from reporting contained in the original article.

## 15. Content Types, Labels, and Disclosures

AI Systems must not remove, conceal, misrepresent, or alter the content type, author, provider, source attribution, or disclosure displayed on a page.

This requirement includes labels identifying:

* reported journalism;
* analysis;
* opinion or commentary;
* external contributions;
* company-provided materials;
* press releases;
* advertisements;
* sponsored content;
* partnership content;
* branded content;
* corrections;
* updates;
* withdrawals; and
* deleted material.

Company-provided materials, advertisements, sponsored content, partnership content, branded content, and press releases must not be presented as independently reported journalism.

Opinion, commentary, or analysis must not be presented as objective factual reporting.

## 16. Corrections, Updates, Deletions, and Freshness

Systems using Maeil Business Newspaper Content must make reasonable efforts to reflect the current status of the original source.

Where an article has been:

* corrected;
* updated;
* withdrawn;
* restricted;
* replaced; or
* marked as deleted,

the relevant status must be reflected in subsequent answers and retrieval results.

When describing current facts, the most recent corrected or updated version should be prioritized.

When describing reporting from a past point in time, the system must clearly distinguish between:

* the content of the article at that time; and
* any subsequent correction, update, retraction, deletion, restriction, or material change.

A cached or indexed copy must not be presented as current where the system knows or reasonably should know that the original article has materially changed.

Systems should implement reasonable processes to refresh, suppress, or delete stale records.

## 17. Third-Party Materials

The permissions granted under this policy apply only to Content for which Maeil Business Newspaper has the authority to authorize the relevant use.

The following materials may be subject to separate rights or restrictions:

* photographs;
* illustrations;
* videos;
* audio;
* wire-service or news-agency articles;
* syndicated reports;
* external contributions;
* guest columns;
* licensed databases;
* financial and market data;
* research reports;
* charts or graphics supplied by third parties;
* corporate filings;
* external documents;
* embedded materials; and
* partner-provided content.

Page-level credits, copyright notices, source labels, license statements, and usage restrictions must be respected.

The presence of third-party material on a Maeil Business Newspaper page does not mean that Maeil Business Newspaper grants permission for AI use of that material.

Where a system cannot determine whether material is owned by or licensed from a third party, it must not assume that the material is covered by the permissions granted under this policy.

## 18. Security, Rate Limits, and Operational Integrity

Automated systems must access Maeil Business Newspaper services in a manner that does not interfere with their availability, security, integrity, or performance.

Systems must:

* comply with applicable rate limits;
* use accurate and non-deceptive user-agent identification;
* avoid excessive or unnecessarily repetitive requests;
* avoid requests that impose unreasonable load;
* respect server responses and access restrictions;
* comply with applicable API conditions;
* avoid circumventing anti-bot or security controls; and
* maintain reasonable security measures for temporarily stored Content and derived data.

Systems must not:

* conduct denial-of-service activity;
* probe for vulnerabilities without authorization;
* evade blocking or throttling;
* rotate identities or network addresses to bypass limits;
* use compromised accounts or credentials;
* access non-public administrative or system endpoints; or
* interfere with authentication, security, tracking, or rights-management systems.

## 19. Prohibited Uses

Except for Model Training expressly covered by prior written authorization or a license as described in Section 7, the following uses are prohibited under this policy:

* Model Training without the required prior written authorization or license;
* creation of a general-purpose dataset;
* creation of a commercial content dataset;
* creation of a competing news, financial, or business-information database;
* permanent storage of full articles;
* reproduction of articles in full or substantially in full;
* systematic archive reconstruction;
* bulk extraction of articles or metadata;
* redistribution or syndication of Content;
* sale or licensing of Content;
* sale or licensing of Indexes, Caches, Embeddings, or derived datasets;
* transfer of Content, Indexes, Caches, Embeddings, or derived datasets to a third party;
* creation of a service that substitutes for Maeil Business Newspaper;
* removal or concealment of source attribution;
* removal or alteration of advertising, sponsorship, branded-content, provider, or source disclosures;
* circumvention of access controls, paywalls, authentication procedures, technical protection measures, or usage limits;
* unauthorized use of Restricted Content;
* fabrication or alteration of quotations, data, statistics, names, dates, or other facts;
* use of Content in a deceptive, misleading, defamatory, or unlawful manner;
* false representation of endorsement, affiliation, sponsorship, or approval by Maeil Business Newspaper;
* impersonation of Maeil Business Newspaper, its journalists, or its services;
* creation of counterfeit articles or pages designed to appear as official Maeil Business Newspaper Content; and
* any use that infringes copyright, database rights, contractual rights, privacy rights, publicity rights, trademarks, or other applicable rights.

## 20. Data Retention and Deletion

Content and derived representations used for a permitted Search and RAG Use must not be retained longer than reasonably necessary for that permitted purpose.

Systems should maintain documented processes for:

* retention periods;
* deletion;
* suppression;
* correction;
* access control;
* security incidents;
* separation of permitted search data from prohibited training data; and
* removal of Content that is deleted, restricted, or no longer publicly available.

Full articles must not be stored permanently.

When Content is no longer reasonably necessary for a permitted Search and RAG Use, the Content and any representations capable of reproducing or reconstructing a meaningful portion of that Content must be securely deleted or rendered unusable.

## 21. Licensing and Written Authorization Requests

Organizations seeking prior written authorization or a license for AI Model Training may contact:

MK AX AI Policy Team
Email: [policy@mkinternet.com](mailto:policy@mkinternet.com)

Requests should include, where applicable:

* the requesting organization’s legal name;
* the relevant service, product, model, crawler, or system;
* the proposed training or development purpose;
* the categories and volume of Content requested;
* the intended storage period;
* the geographic scope;
* the intended outputs;
* the proposed security controls;
* the proposed deletion controls; and
* business and technical contact details.

No authorization or license is granted unless and until Maeil Business Newspaper provides express written approval.

Silence, failure to respond, technical access, prior access, or the absence of a technical block does not constitute authorization or a license.

Any authorization or license applies only according to its express written terms and does not modify this policy for any other person, organization, service, system, model, crawler, or use.

## 22. Enforcement and Reservation of Rights

Maeil Business Newspaper reserves all rights not expressly granted under this policy.

Where Maeil Business Newspaper reasonably believes that a system or organization has violated this policy, it may take measures including:

* restricting or blocking automated access;
* revoking technical access;
* requesting deletion of improperly obtained Content;
* requesting deletion of unauthorized Indexes, Caches, Embeddings, or datasets;
* contacting the relevant service provider, platform, network, or infrastructure operator;
* pursuing contractual remedies; and
* pursuing any other available legal or technical remedy.

Failure to enforce a provision in one instance does not constitute a waiver of that provision or of Maeil Business Newspaper’s rights.

Nothing in this policy limits any rights or remedies available under applicable law, contract, copyright, database rights, unfair-competition rules, technical-protection laws, or other legal principles.

## 23. No Transfer of Ownership

No permission granted under this policy transfers ownership of any Content, intellectual property right, database right, trademark, or other proprietary right.

All ownership and rights remain with Maeil Business Newspaper or the applicable third-party rights holder.

Permitted use of a source name or link for attribution does not grant permission to use Maeil Business Newspaper’s trademarks, logos, trade dress, or branding for marketing, endorsement, or promotional purposes.

## 24. No Warranty or Endorsement

Content is provided as published and may be updated, corrected, restricted, or removed.

To the extent permitted by applicable law, Maeil Business Newspaper does not guarantee that Content will be:

* complete;
* current;
* error-free;
* continuously available; or
* suitable for a particular automated use.

Permission to use Public Free Content under this policy does not constitute endorsement of any AI System, product, provider, answer, analysis, or output.

AI providers remain responsible for the operation of their systems and for the accuracy, legality, security, and presentation of their outputs.

## 25. Changes to This Policy

Maeil Business Newspaper may update this policy to reflect changes in:

* technology;
* services;
* content offerings;
* crawler practices;
* licensing arrangements;
* legal requirements; or
* business policies.

Changes will be reflected by updating the “Last Updated” date and, where appropriate, the policy version.

Continued access or use after an update is subject to the revised policy from its effective date.

## 26. Interpretation

Headings are provided for convenience and do not limit the meaning of any provision.

Examples in this policy are illustrative and not exhaustive.

The terms “including” and “includes” mean “including without limitation.”

Where this policy refers to prior written authorization or a license, electronic contracts and formal written communications issued by an authorized representative of Maeil Business Newspaper may qualify, subject to their express terms.

Where a translated version of this policy is provided, the version identified by Maeil Business Newspaper as controlling will govern in the event of an inconsistency.

